  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe ridge aloft will take on a northeast-southwest orientation and \nshift to the west Thursday into Saturday. The ridge will be centered \nover Arkansas on Thursday, the ArkLaTex on Friday, and the ArkLaTex \nand eastern Texas on Saturday. Hot and dry conditions will persist \nThursday into Saturday. This ridge will not be quite as strong late \nthis week. Our temperatures will be a little less hot but still well-\nabove normal. Expect highs in the mid to upper 90s for our southern \ncounties, and 96-100 degrees across the central and northern \ncounties.  \n\nChanges in the pattern are indicated over the weekend and early next \nweek. An upper trough is forecast to move east across the Rockies \nSaturday night and early Sunday, and across the Great Plains Sunday \nafternoon and Sunday night. This will weaken the aforementioned \nridge. Far western and northwestern parts of our area will have a \nlow chance (15-20 percent) of showers and thunderstorms Saturday \nnight. A mostly low chance (20-30 percent) of showers and storms \nwill expand across the northwest half of our area Sunday. Expect \nhighs Sunday to be generally in the mid to upper 90s. \n\nMedium range models have either a weak ridge or weakness aloft over \nour area early next week. A weak cold front may sag south of the Red \nRiver and into our northern counties on Monday. With precipitable \nwater values progged to be at or above 1.5 inches across some of our \narea, showers and thunderstorms will be possible but there is \nuncertainty in coverage and rain chances. Blended models at this \ntime have medium rain chances (30-50 percent) Monday, and low to \nmedium (20-40 percent) chances Monday night and Tuesday. \nTemperatures (how hot) also look uncertain with the latest model \ntrends a little higher for highs Monday and Tuesday (in the lower to \nmid 90s). Now looking like the better chance of having highs below \n90 would be in the Big Country north of where the front can make \nsouthward progress.",
